\begin{proof}
Let $\mathcal T_\ell$ and $\mathcal T_K$ be the lower-limit and $K$-topologies on $\mathbb R$, where $K=\{1/n:n\in\mathbb N\}$. The set $[0,1)$ is $\mathcal T_\ell$-open. If a $\mathcal T_K$-basis element $(a,b)\setminus K$ contains $0$, then $a<0<b$, so it contains negative numbers and cannot be contained in $[0,1)$. Hence $[0,1)\notin\mathcal T_K$, and $\mathcal T_\ell\not\subseteq\mathcal T_K$.

Conversely, $(-1,1)\setminus K$ is $\mathcal T_K$-open and contains $0$. Every lower-limit basis element containing $0$ has the form $[0,b)$ with $b>0$, and therefore contains some $1/n\in K$ with $1/n<b$. Thus no such basis element is contained in $(-1,1)\setminus K$. Hence $(-1,1)\setminus K\notin\mathcal T_\ell$, so $\mathcal T_K\not\subseteq\mathcal T_\ell$. Therefore the two topologies are not comparable.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Write $K=\{1/n:n\in\mathbb N\}$. Let $\mathcal T_\ell$ be generated by the basis $[a,b)$ and let $\mathcal T_K$ be generated by $(a,b)\setminus K$.

The set $[0,1)$ belongs to $\mathcal T_\ell$. Any $\mathcal T_K$-basic neighborhood of $0$ is $(a,b)\setminus K$ with $a<0<b$, and hence contains points less than $0$. It cannot be contained in $[0,1)$. Thus $\mathcal T_\ell\not\subseteq\mathcal T_K$.

The set $U=(-1,1)\setminus K$ belongs to $\mathcal T_K$. If $[a,b)$ is a lower-limit basic neighborhood of $0$, then $a\le 0<b$. If $a<0$, it contains negative points and is not contained in $U$; if $a=0$, choose $n$ with $1/n<b$, so $1/n\in[a,b)\cap K$, again preventing containment in $U$. Hence $U$ is not $\mathcal T_\ell$-open. Therefore $\mathcal T_K\not\subseteq\mathcal T_\ell$.

Consequently, $\mathcal T_\ell$ and $\mathcal T_K$ are not comparable.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Let $K=\{1/n:n\in\mathbb N\}$. The lower-limit topology $\mathcal T_\ell$ has basis $\{[a,b):a<b\}$, while the $K$-topology $\mathcal T_K$ has basis consisting of ordinary open intervals together with sets $(a,b)\setminus K$.

First, $[0,1)\in\mathcal T_\ell$. Suppose that a $\mathcal T_K$-basic neighborhood of $0$ were contained in $[0,1)$. An ordinary interval containing $0$ contains negative points, and a set $(a,b)\setminus K$ containing $0$ also has $a<0$, hence contains negative points. This is impossible. Thus $[0,1)\notin\mathcal T_K$, proving $\mathcal T_\ell\not\subseteq\mathcal T_K$.

Next, $V=(-1,1)\setminus K\in\mathcal T_K$. If $V$ were lower-limit open, it would contain a basis set $[a,b)$ with $0\in[a,b)\subseteq V$. Necessarily $a\le0<b$. When $a<0$, the set contains a negative real, which is not in $V$. When $a=0$, choose $n$ sufficiently large that $1/n<b$; then $1/n\in[a,b)$ but $1/n\notin V$. Both cases are contradictions. Therefore $V\notin\mathcal T_\ell$, so $\mathcal T_K\not\subseteq\mathcal T_\ell$.

Hence the topologies are not comparable.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Let $K=\{1/n:n\in\mathbb N\}$. Denote the lower-limit topology by $\mathcal T_\ell$ and the $K$-topology by $\mathcal T_K$.

To show $\mathcal T_\ell\not\subseteq\mathcal T_K$, consider the lower-limit basic open set $A=[0,1)$. If $B$ is any $\mathcal T_K$-basic neighborhood of $0$, then either $B$ is an ordinary open interval containing $0$, or $B=(a,b)\setminus K$ with $a<0<b$. In either case $B$ contains a negative real number, so $B\nsubseteq A$. Therefore $A$ is not $\mathcal T_K$-open.

To show $\mathcal T_K\not\subseteq\mathcal T_\ell$, consider $C=(-1,1)\setminus K$, which is $\mathcal T_K$-open. If $C$ were lower-limit open, there would be a lower-limit basic neighborhood $[a,b)$ of $0$ contained in $C$. Since $0\in[a,b)$, we have $a\le0<b$. If $a<0$, then $[a,b)$ contains a negative point outside $C$. If $a=0$, choose $n$ with $1/n<b$; then $1/n\in[0,b)$ but $1/n\in K$, so it is not in $C$. Either way, containment fails. Thus $C$ is not lower-limit open.

Both inclusions fail, and consequently $\mathbb R_\ell$ and $\mathbb R_K$ are not comparable.
\end{proof}
